mation Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni Open search input Jobs at King’s College London Your career Navigation link How we recruit Navigation link Our benefits Navigation link Our culture Navigation link About King's Navigation link Payroll Administrator Job id: 160285. Salary: £34,713 - £38,127 per annum inclusive of London Weighting Allowance. Posted: 12 October 2026. Closing date: 25 October 2026. Business unit: Finance Procurement Analytics. Department: Payroll & Pensions Services. Contact details: Christopher Zacharia.  Christopher.zacharia@kcl.ac.uk Location: Guy's Campus. Category: Professional & Support Services. Back to job search About us: King's College London is a world-leading university with a strong commitment to excellence in education, research and professional services. The Payroll & Pensions team sits within Finance, Procurement & Analytics and provides a vital service to employees across the University. As part of a supportive and collaborative team, you will help deliver accurate, timely and compliant payroll services to a large and diverse workforce. The team is responsible for processing payroll changes, maintaining employee records, supporting payroll enquiries and ensuring payroll activities are completed in line with statutory requirements, University policies and payroll deadlines. Working closely with colleagues across Payroll & Pensions, Human Resources and other departments, you will contribute to providing a professional, customer-focused service while developing valuable knowledge and experience within payroll administration. We are committed to continuous improvement, teamwork and service excellence, creating an environment where colleagues are supported to learn, develop and build rewarding careers. About the role: We are seeking a Payroll Administrator to join the Payroll & Pensions team and support the delivery of accurate, timely and compliant payroll services across King's College London. In this role, you will be responsible for administering payroll activity for a designated employee population, ensuring payroll changes are processed accurately and in line with payroll deadlines, statutory requirements and University policies. Working as part of a collaborative team, you will process a range of payroll transactions, including starters, leavers, contractual changes, absence-related pay adjustments and one-off payments. You will maintain accurate records, help resolve payroll queries and contribute to providing a professional and customer-focused service to employees and stakeholders across the University. This role would suit someone with strong organisational skills, a high level of accuracy and attention to detail, and the ability to manage competing priorities in a busy environment. You will work closely with colleagues across Payroll & Pensions, Human Resources and other departments, gaining valuable experience within a specialist payroll function while contributing to a service that supports employees throughout the University. The successful candidate will be confident handling sensitive and confidential information, committed to delivering excellent customer service, and motivated to learn and develop within a supportive team environment. We welcome applications from candidates with relevant administrative, customer service, finance, HR or payroll experience who are keen to build a career in payroll administration. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). The successful candidate will be offered a fixed-term contract for 12 months. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Educated to GCSE standard (or equivalent), including Mathematics and English Knowledge of payroll legislation and statutory requirements, including PAYE, National Insurance, pensions and statutory payments. Strong organisational and time management skills with ability to work proactively as part of a team Strong attention to detail with the ability to maintain accuracy when working with high volumes of data and deadlines Ability to analyse information and perform numerical calculations accurately Experience of working in an administrative or data-processing environment with competing priorities and deadlines Ability to communicate effectively and provide professional customer-focused support when responding to queries Ability to handle confidential information appropriately and work in accordance with data protection, audit requirements and internal controls and policies Desirable criteria Proficient in Microsoft Office applications, particularly Excel, Outlook and Word Experience of working in a payroll, HR, finance or similar administrative environment. CIPP Qualification, or working towards a recognised payroll qualification Experience of University or public sector payroll & pension arrangements Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘How we Recruit’ pages. About us: King's College London is a world-leading university with a strong commitment to excellence in education, research and professional services. The Payroll & Pensions team sits within Finance, Procurement & Analytics and provides a vital service to employees across the University. As part of a supportive and collaborative team, you will help deliver accurate, timely and compliant payroll services to a large and diverse workforce. The team is responsible for processing payroll changes, maintaining employee records, supporting payroll enquiries and ensuring payroll activities are completed in line with statutory requirements, University policies and payroll deadlines. Working closely with colleagues across Payroll & Pensions, Human Resources and other departments, you will contribute to providing a professional, customer-focused service while developing valuable knowledge and experience within payroll administration. We are committed to continuous improvement, teamwork and service excellence, creating an environment where colleagues are supported to learn, develop and build rewarding careers. About the role: We are seeking a Payroll Administrator to join the Payroll & Pensions team and support the delivery of accurate, timely and compliant payroll services across King's College London. In this role, you will be responsible for administering payroll activity for a designated employee population, ensuring payroll changes are processed accurately and in line with payroll deadlines, statutory requirements and University policies. Working as part of a collaborative team, you will process a range of payroll transactions, including starters, leavers, contractual changes, absence-related pay adjustments and one-off payments. You will maintain accurate records, help resolve payroll queries and contribute to providing a professional and customer-focused service to employees and stakeholders across the University. This role would suit someone with strong organisational skills, a high level of accuracy and attention to detail, and the ability to manage competing priorities in a busy environment. You will work closely with colleagues across Payroll & Pensions, Human Resources and other departments, gaining valuable experience within a specialist payroll function while contributing to a service that supports employees throughout the University. The successful candidate will be confident handling sensitive and confidential information, committed to delivering excellent customer service, and motivated to learn and develop within a supportive team environment. We welcome applications from candidates with relevant administrative, customer service, finance, HR or payroll experience who are keen to build a career in payroll administration. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). The successful candidate will be offered a fixed-term contract for 12 months. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Educated to GCSE standard (or equivalent), including Mathematics and English Knowledge of payroll legislation and statutory requirements, including PAYE, National Insurance, pensions and statutory payments. Strong organisational and time management skills with ability to work proactively as part of a team Strong attention to detail with the ability to maintain accuracy when working with high volumes of data and deadlines Ability to analyse information and perform numerical calculations accurately Experience of working in an administrative or data-processing environment with competing priorities and deadlines Ability to communicate effectively and provide professional customer-focused support when responding to queries Ability to handle confidential information appropriately and work in accordance with data protection, audit requirements and internal controls and policies Desirable criteria Proficient in Microsoft Office applications, particularly Excel, Outlook and Word Experience of working in a payroll, HR, finance or similar administrative environment. CIPP Qualification, or working towards a recognised payroll qualification Experience of University or public sector payroll & pension arrangements Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapi"
